{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"=== HANDOFF #18 ADDENDUM 3 — `recheck-review` HAS A SIDE EFFECT: it also re-runs CI and CLEARS Verified ===\n\n**The LLM review bot can silently skip a change entirely.** Changes 1279 and 1283 sat with `Verified +1`\nand NO LLM-Review vote and — the diagnostic detail — **no review message from the bot at all**, while\n1276 and 1280 (pushed in the same window) each got theirs. So it is not a BLOCK or an error; the bot\nsimply never processed them. Check the message list, not just the label: \"no vote\" and \"voted but\nunhappy\" look identical in the label alone.\n\n**The documented remedy is to comment `recheck-review`** (distinct from `recheck`, which re-runs CI /\n`Verified` only). That mechanism already works server-side; the change that merely DOCUMENTS it in the\ncontributor guide (1258, ticket f418) is itself still in review, which is why `grep recheck-review` over\n`src/rebar/_guides/` finds nothing — read the change, not the guide.\n\n**THE TRAP: `recheck-review` ALSO TRIGGERS CI.** `gerrit-verify.yaml:6` says the workflow is dispatched\nby gerrit-to-platform \"on `patchset-created` and on a **`recheck` comment**\" — and the string\n\"recheck-review\" CONTAINS \"recheck\", so it matches that trigger too. Measured on 1279: posting\n`recheck-review` produced `Patch Set 2: -Verified  STARTED: .../runs/30784911973`, i.e. it **cleared an\nexisting `Verified +1`** and re-ran the whole suite. Consequences to plan around:\n * you lose a good `Verified` vote for the length of a CI cycle, so a change that was submittable is\n   temporarily not;\n * on a STACK this compounds — each `recheck-review` costs that change's `Verified` too, so do not fire\n   them across a stack at once;\n * and it interacts with the #15 concurrency-group hazard: extra dispatches are extra chances for two runs\n   to cancel each other. SERIALIZE them.\n\n**If `recheck-review` does not produce an LLM vote, the fallback is a no-op `git commit --amend --no-edit`\nre-push.** `patchset-created` triggers BOTH bots, so it reliably gets the LLM review — and it costs a CI\nre-run either way, which `recheck-review` already cost. Keep the Change-Id (never `--amend -F -`, which\nregenerates it and forks a duplicate change)."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785731943425525001,"uuid":"f5ef7140-bdc3-40ec-9bef-c81aa5a32db9"}